WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:03.350
INTRO:
Starbucks isn’t doing that great. In fact,

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:10.260
it’s down 40% year to date and it’s currently
the 474th worst performing stock in the S&amp;P

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:17.430
500. This correlates to a total market cap
loss of $66.4 billion. Given how the overall

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:22.540
market is doing though, I bet a lot of you
guys aren’t even surprised. The NASDAQ and

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:28.480
Russell 2000 are officially in bear markets,
and the S&amp;P 500 is teetering between correction

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:33.370
and bear market territory. But, while it’s
easy to just write off Starbucks’ sell off

00:00:33.370 --> 00:00:37.860
as a result of a weak market, there’s actually
a lot of background factors that are prompting

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:43.250
this sell of. And if you don’t believe me,
just take a look at this. Last month, the

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:47.560
retired father of Starbucks, Howard Shultz,
returned to Starbucks to change the direction

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:52.010
of the company. This isn’t the first time
Howard is leaving retirement to run Starbucks

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:57.130
either. Back in 2008, during the height of
the financial recession, Howard returned to

00:00:57.130 --> 00:01:02.920
Starbucks as CEO and he led the company up
until 2017. During this time period, Starbucks

00:01:02.920 --> 00:01:08.120
stock grew an impressive 17X from the 2008
bottom, but the company hasn’t down all

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:13.090
that much since Howard left. In fact, the
stock is almost back to the same prices that

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:18.530
he left it. This has caused many investors
to question if anybody can run Starbucks other

00:01:18.530 --> 00:01:23.230
than Howard. But, before we get into that,
we have to look into whether Howard can even

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:28.680
save Starbucks again in the first place. So,
what even happened to Starbucks?

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:35.350
SHORTAGES:
One of the top factors driving down Starbucks

00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:40.190
is worldwide shortages in almost everything
starting with labor. If you haven’t heard

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:44.479
already, a significant number of employees
especially in the US have been quitting their

00:01:44.479 --> 00:01:50.510
jobs over the past few years. In fact, in
2021, 47 million Americans voluntarily quit

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:56.729
their jobs. And, if you didn’t notice, that’s
literally ¼ of the entire US labor force.

00:01:56.729 --> 00:02:01.310
Given that the unemployment rate isn’t skryrocketing
though, it’s clear that these guys aren’t

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:05.280
just leaving the corporate world altogether.
Rather, they’re switching over to companies

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:10.259
and jobs where they feel more respected and
appreciated. Naturally, you would think that

00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:16.769
compensation plays a big factor in this decision
as well, but this is actually not the case.

00:02:16.769 --> 00:02:21.219
In fact, dissatisfaction with one’s salary
does not even rank in the top ten reasons

00:02:21.219 --> 00:02:27.489
that people are leaving the jobs. Salary dissatisfaction
actually comes in at rank 16. Some of the

00:02:27.489 --> 00:02:32.260
reasons that people are really switching jobs
is a toxic corporate culture and underappreciated

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:36.799
work. And there’s no question that these
issues are especially prevalent within service

00:02:36.799 --> 00:02:41.470
oriented industries like Starbucks. This trend
has made it extremely difficult for Starbucks

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:46.609
to fill positions and keep them filled. And
it’s not just a case of oh we’re missing

00:02:46.609 --> 00:02:50.919
a cashier today or we’re missing a barista
today. No, Starbucks is literally missing

00:02:50.919 --> 00:02:55.480
half of their workforce. You see, when the
pandemic hit, Starbucks laid off the vast

00:02:55.480 --> 00:03:01.909
majority of their employees. Their employee
count fell from 349,000 employees to 138,000

00:03:01.909 --> 00:03:06.680
employees. Meanwhile, the number of stores
Starbucks was operating never went down, it

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:11.470
actually kept going up. We don’t yet know
how many of these positions Starbucks has

00:03:11.470 --> 00:03:16.290
filled since last September, but I think it’s
pretty safe to assume that it’s not the

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:21.279
full 211,000 positions. This means that the
remaining employees are having to do even

00:03:21.279 --> 00:03:25.969
more work which is just further motivating
them to switch jobs. The staffing shortage

00:03:25.969 --> 00:03:31.480
has actually gotten so bad that some Starbucks
locations have starting reducing store hours,

00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:36.260
and as a business, that’s usually the last
thing you want to do especially if there’s

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:41.010
sufficient demand. Aside from facing a severe
staffing shortage, Starbucks is also having

00:03:41.010 --> 00:03:45.099
to deal with a supply shortage. If you go
to your local Starbucks, it’s more than

00:03:45.099 --> 00:03:50.019
likely that atleast a few of their menu items
are unavailable due to a lack of ingredients.

00:03:50.019 --> 00:03:54.529
Some of the hardest hit ingredients include
oat milk, flavored syrups, and tea bags, but

00:03:54.529 --> 00:03:59.719
the shortage also extends to some of Starbuck’s
most popular offerings as well. Some locations

00:03:59.719 --> 00:04:04.900
don’t even have frappuccino mix or breakfast
sandwiches. And just a few months, the entire

00:04:04.900 --> 00:04:09.760
region of upstate New York ran out of mocha.
So, not only are Starbuck’s employees having

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:13.709
to do more work, but they’re also having
to constantly deal with customers that are

00:04:13.709 --> 00:04:18.050
upset that they can’t even get what they
came for. And the longer these shortages keep

00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:23.900
up, it’s just gonna become harder and harder
for Starbucks to recover.

00:04:23.900 --> 00:04:28.290
INFLATION:
The supply shortage isn’t the only reason

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:33.700
that customers are upset either. Another issue
that is driving customer dissatisfaction is

00:04:33.700 --> 00:04:38.590
rising prices. Now, it’s no secret that
Starbucks is already a premium brand with

00:04:38.590 --> 00:04:43.690
expensive offerings. Many people have looked
past these prices for years due to convenience

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:48.680
and taste. However, as Starbucks continues
to raise prices, it’s becoming harder and

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:54.140
harder for customers to justify going to Starbucks.
The worst part is that this is not something

00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:59.240
that Starbucks even has that much control
over. Prices across the board are simply skyrocketing,

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:03.750
and they have to raise prices to keep up.
For example, the price of coffee has doubled

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:09.851
since 2019 from $100 a pound to over $200
per pound. Similarly, I’m sure all of the

00:05:09.851 --> 00:05:13.500
equipment at Starbuck’s locations along
with their cups and spoons and straws have

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:18.620
also risen in price due to out of control
inflation. And on top of all this, Starbucks

00:05:18.620 --> 00:05:23.700
is having to raise wages to retain the employees
that they do have. Last October, they committed

00:05:23.700 --> 00:05:28.970
to increasing minimum wage across the country
to $15 per hour. Now, I’m not trying argue

00:05:28.970 --> 00:05:34.070
whether such wage hikes are justified or not.
But, one thing that’s certain is that someone

00:05:34.070 --> 00:05:38.880
has to pay for these higher wages, and more
times than not, that someone is the customer.

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:42.560
Meanwhile, from the customers’ perspective,
it’s not just Starbucks where they’re

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:47.160
having to spend more money. Over the past
year, their cost of living across the board

00:05:47.160 --> 00:05:50.860
has skyrocketed. Everything from gas and energy
bills to groceries and housing costs have

00:05:50.860 --> 00:05:55.470
gone to the moon while wages haven’t. And
there’s only so many prices increases that

00:05:55.470 --> 00:05:59.830
consumers can afford before they start to
decrease their consumption. Necessities like

00:05:59.830 --> 00:06:04.940
gas and groceries are something you can’t
avoid, but something you can avoid is overpriced

00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:09.860
Starbucks coffee. And as inflation continues
to remain high, we’ll likely just see more

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:12.610
and more people cut back on such purchases.

00:06:12.610 --> 00:06:17.890
BOTCHED ONLINE SYSTEM:
Something else that’s also leading to customer

00:06:17.890 --> 00:06:23.470
and employee dissatisfaction is Starbuck’s
mobile/online order system. Embracing a mobile

00:06:23.470 --> 00:06:28.150
order system was a no brainer during the pandemic,
but long term it seems to have caused more

00:06:28.150 --> 00:06:32.710
headaches than solutions. Here’s the thing,
when people come to the store and order in

00:06:32.710 --> 00:06:37.460
person, there’s a natural limit to how long
the line will get. I mean if there’s already

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:41.720
20 people in line, people who come after are
more likely to just skip Starbucks for the

00:06:41.720 --> 00:06:46.640
day. But, when a significant number of people
are ordering online and there’s a long line

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:51.150
at the store, fulfilling all these orders
quickly becomes overwhelming especially during

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:55.810
rush hours with a staffing shortage. And when
these online customers show up at the store,

00:06:55.810 --> 00:07:00.080
they’re often upset that their order isn’t
ready yet. Something else to note is that

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:04.130
when people order online, they’re way more
likely to make more complex orders because

00:07:04.130 --> 00:07:08.920
it’s easier to click buttons than to verbally
communicate such an order. This has made fulfilling

00:07:08.920 --> 00:07:13.090
online orders more time consuming which has
just made it even harder to keep up with rush

00:07:13.090 --> 00:07:18.400
hour demand. To make things worse, their online
system often doesn’t reflect missing ingredients

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:22.910
meaning that customers are often allowed put
in orders that can’t be made. This leaves

00:07:22.910 --> 00:07:27.320
the employees with two options. Either they
can inform the customer that their order can’t

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:31.050
be completed when they show up at the store
which is probably not the most fun thing to

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:36.420
do. Or, they can substitute the missing ingredient
with an alternative that is available. But,

00:07:36.420 --> 00:07:40.720
this doesn’t necessarily lead to the happiest
of customers either. The straightforward solution

00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:45.950
to this conundrum is for Starbucks to revamp
their online system and implement two features.

00:07:45.950 --> 00:07:49.950
First of all, their online system should limit
online orders to a manageable volume, and

00:07:49.950 --> 00:07:54.710
secondly, their online system should accurately
represent shortages. If they don’t revamp

00:07:54.710 --> 00:07:59.060
their online system, we’ll likely just continue
to see more misunderstandings between customers

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:03.960
and employees causing Starbucks to lose customers
over the long term.

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:08.150
UNIONS:
While all of the factors that we’ve discussed

00:08:08.150 --> 00:08:13.120
so far are definitely weighing down on Starbucks’
business. Likely the biggest reason behind

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:18.150
Howard’s return is Starbucks employees unionizing.
For service based businesses with hundreds

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:23.250
of thousands of minimum wage workers, unions
are often their biggest fear. Here’s the

00:08:23.250 --> 00:08:27.340
thing, while Starbucks charges a ridiculous
amount for their beverages, they don’t actually

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:32.610
take much of this to the bottom line. Historically,
Starbucks’ net margin has hovered at just

00:08:32.610 --> 00:08:37.920
under 15%. And while this a solid amount for
a restaurant business, this doesn’t give

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:42.810
them much leeway in terms of raising wages.
So, Starbucks and other low margin companies

00:08:42.810 --> 00:08:48.410
like Amazon are extremely anti union. But,
despite their attempts to stifle unions, we’ve

00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:53.149
seen Starbucks employees put up quite a fight
over the past year. For example, remember

00:08:53.149 --> 00:08:57.720
how Starbucks committed to increasing their
minimum wage to $15 per hour. Well, this was

00:08:57.720 --> 00:09:02.280
a last ditch effort to try to stifle union
participation. Before they hiked wages, they

00:09:02.280 --> 00:09:06.620
were attempting to terminate every union member
they could find. They even sent managers to

00:09:06.620 --> 00:09:11.569
Starbucks locations to try to dissuade union
participation. And it looks like Starbuck’s

00:09:11.569 --> 00:09:16.410
is simply doubling down on crushing unions.
At the beginning of May, Howard announced

00:09:16.410 --> 00:09:21.410
a set of raises that will only benefit non
unionized workers. And he justified this decision

00:09:21.410 --> 00:09:26.519
with likely the most garbage reason you have
ever heard. Howard suggested that he couldn’t

00:09:26.519 --> 00:09:31.230
raise wages for unionized workers because
that would be imposing new wages without the

00:09:31.230 --> 00:09:35.620
union’s approval. Starbucks Workers United
has fired back by filing charges against Howard

00:09:35.620 --> 00:09:40.660
for discriminating against Union workers.
This is still a very fluid situation, so we’ll

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:45.019
have to wait and see how this battle plays
out. But honestly, I think Howard’s best

00:09:45.019 --> 00:09:49.579
choice is to simply give in to the Unions.
Here’s the thing, Howard can play this game

00:09:49.579 --> 00:09:54.519
as much as he wants and he might even win
against Unions, but I think Howard’s victory

00:09:54.519 --> 00:09:59.670
would actually be a loss for Starbucks. Over
the years, Starbucks has maintained a pretty

00:09:59.670 --> 00:10:04.070
respectable image when it comes to employee
benefits and treatment. But, pushing vigorously

00:10:04.070 --> 00:10:08.529
against unions can easily destroy that image
and I don’t think that’s in Starbucks’

00:10:08.529 --> 00:10:13.110
best interest. Given that this is the primary
reason that Howard has returned though, I

00:10:13.110 --> 00:10:14.583
suspect that this is going to be bloody battle.

00:10:14.583 --> 00:10:21.910
THE STATE OF STARBUCKS:
At the end of the day, Starbucks is a mess

00:10:21.910 --> 00:10:26.420
both internally and externally. They’re
plagued with staff and supply shortages while

00:10:26.420 --> 00:10:30.990
having to deal with insane inflation and bad
software which is leading to pretty unhappy

00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:36.230
customers. On top of this, Starbucks is ferociously
fighting against internal unionization efforts

00:10:36.230 --> 00:10:40.990
which have thus far proven quite successful.
Howard returning to Starbucks simply enunciates

00:10:40.990 --> 00:10:47.019
how critical the situation is. And unfortunately,
there is no easy solution. If Howard gives

00:10:47.019 --> 00:10:51.269
into the union, Starbucks will likely have
to deal with even higher operating expenses

00:10:51.269 --> 00:10:55.420
which will just make even harder for them
to retain customers. On the other hand, if

00:10:55.420 --> 00:10:59.209
Howard doesn’t give in, Starbucks will not
only continue to face a staffing shortage

00:10:59.209 --> 00:11:04.860
but they’ll probably tarnish their reputation.
And in my opinion, their long term reputation

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:09.999
is worth a lot more than short term profits.
But with that being said, if anyone can get

00:11:09.999 --> 00:11:14.730
Starbucks back on track, it’s Howard. So,
we’ll have to wait and see what he does.

00:11:14.730 --> 00:11:19.579
